{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"container-adaptors","title":"stack, queue & priority_queue","group":"STL Containers & Iterators","order":54,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Container adaptors wrap a sequence container and expose only a restricted interface: LIFO (<code>stack</code>), FIFO (<code>queue</code>) or “largest first” (<code>priority_queue</code>).</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-push\"></i><code>std::stack</code> &amp; <code>std::queue</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nbool balanced(const std::string&amp; s) {\n    std::stack&lt;char&gt; st;\n    for (char c : s) {\n        if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') st.push(c);\n        else {\n            if (st.empty()) return false;\n            char open = st.top();\n            st.pop();\n            if ((c == ')' &amp;&amp; open != '(') || (c == ']' &amp;&amp; open != '[') || (c == '}' &amp;&amp; open != '{')) return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return st.empty();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; balanced(\"{[()]}\") &lt;&lt; balanced(\"([)]\") &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::queue&lt;std::string&gt; q;                  // FIFO\n    q.push(\"first\"); q.push(\"second\"); q.push(\"third\");\n    q.pop();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; q.back() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; q.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10\nsecond third 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Adaptor</th><th>Operations</th><th>Default underlying container</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>stack&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>empty</code>, <code>size</code></td><td><code>deque&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>queue&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>front</code>, <code>back</code>, <code>empty</code>, <code>size</code></td><td><code>deque&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>priority_queue&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>top</code>, <code>empty</code>, <code>size</code></td><td><code>vector&lt;T&gt;</code> (binary heap)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>pop()</code> returns <code>void</code> — read <code>top()</code>/<code>front()</code> first. Calling <code>top()</code>, <code>front()</code> or <code>pop()</code> on an empty adaptor is undefined behaviour. None of them are iterable.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-descending\"></i><code>std::priority_queue</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::priority_queue&lt;int&gt; maxHeap;                                   // largest on top\n    for (int x : {3, 1, 4, 1, 5}) maxHeap.push(x);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; maxHeap.top() &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    maxHeap.pop();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; maxHeap.top() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::priority_queue&lt;int, std::vector&lt;int&gt;, std::greater&lt;int&gt;&gt; minHeap{std::greater&lt;int&gt;(), {3, 1, 4}};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; minHeap.top() &lt;&lt; '\\n';                                 // smallest on top\n\n    using Task = std::pair&lt;int, std::string&gt;;                           // (priority, name)\n    auto cmp = [](const Task&amp; a, const Task&amp; b) { return a.first &gt; b.first; };   // lower number = more urgent\n    std::priority_queue&lt;Task, std::vector&lt;Task&gt;, decltype(cmp)&gt; tasks(cmp);\n    tasks.push({2, \"email\"}); tasks.push({1, \"outage\"}); tasks.push({3, \"lunch\"});\n    while (!tasks.empty()) { std::cout &lt;&lt; tasks.top().second &lt;&lt; ' '; tasks.pop(); }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 4\n1\noutage email lunch</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Comparator direction</div><p>The comparator says “<code>a</code> has <em>lower</em> priority than <code>b</code>” — so <code>std::less</code> (default) gives a max-heap and <code>std::greater</code> a min-heap. It's the reverse of what you'd expect from sorting.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trophy\"></i>Top-k with a heap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; scores{50, 90, 20, 70, 85, 60};\n    std::priority_queue&lt;int, std::vector&lt;int&gt;, std::greater&lt;int&gt;&gt; top3;   // min-heap of size k\n    for (int s : scores) {\n        top3.push(s);\n        if (top3.size() &gt; 3) top3.pop();       // drop the smallest: O(n log k)\n    }\n    while (!top3.empty()) { std::cout &lt;&lt; top3.top() &lt;&lt; ' '; top3.pop(); }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>70 85 90</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How is priority_queue implemented?</summary><div><p>As a binary heap stored in a <code>std::vector</code>, using <code>std::push_heap</code>/<code>std::pop_heap</code>: <code>top</code> is O(1), <code>push</code> and <code>pop</code> are O(log n), and building from n elements is O(n). It has no decrease-key; Dijkstra implementations push duplicates and skip stale entries instead.</p></div></details></section>"}